.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAM-CREATE-ROLE 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.73" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eam-create-role \- create a role: a named set of permissions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eam create-role
.BR \-\-name | \-n
.I NAME
.BR \-\-permission | \-p
.I PERMISSION
.RI [ PERMISSION ...]
[\fB\-\-description\fR|\fB\-d\fR \fITEXT\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Creates a role in your own account. A role is a named set of permissions
and nothing else \- it says what may be done, never by whom or where.
Those two are said when the role is given to somebody, by
.BR euclid-cli-eam-grant-role (1).
.PP
The separation is the point: a role is written once and granted many
times, each time scoped differently. "What an order service may do" is
one role; the twelve deployments of it are twelve grants.
.SH BEFORE WRITING ONE
Check whether one of the seven built-in roles already says it \-
.BR euclid-cli-eam-list-roles (1)
shows them. They are maintained with the vocabulary, so a module that
gains an action lands in
.I reader
and
.I operator
on the next build, with nobody to remember. A role of your own does not
get that and is yours to keep current.
.SH PERMISSIONS
Each
.B \-\-permission
is one of three forms, and no others:
.TP
.I <module>:<action>
One action, e.g.
.IR ens:publish\-message .
.TP
.I <module>:*
Every action of one module, e.g.
.IR ens:* .
Includes the ones that module gains later.
.TP
.I *:*
Every action of every bindable module. The strongest thing a role can
say \- and still not installation administration, since it does not
reach
.I emm
or
.IR emd .
.PP
Every one is checked against what the modules actually dispatch, so a
role cannot be created holding a permission that grants nothing. A
misspelling is refused here rather than discovered by somebody being
turned away from something they were told they had. See
.BR euclid-cli-eam-list-permissions (1)
for the whole vocabulary.
.PP
A role must hold at least one permission. A role holding none grants
nothing, which is a mistake rather than a configuration.
.SH THE BUILT-IN NAMES ARE TAKEN
.IR account\-administrator ,
.IR operator ,
.IR reader ,
.IR publisher ,
.IR consumer ,
.I application
and
.I transfer
cannot be redefined. A grant resolves your account's own roles before the
built-in ones, so a stored role of that name would silently replace the
built-in for your account alone \- and an installation where
.I reader
means something different in one account is one where nobody can read a
grant and know what it does.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-name " " \fINAME\fR
Role name, unique within your account. Required.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-permission " " \fIPERMISSION\fR
A permission the role grants. Repeat the option, or give several after
one. Required, at least once.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-description " " \fITEXT\fR
What the role is for. Defaults to an empty string.
.SH WHO MAY RUN IT
Installation administrators \- members of the
.I administrator
user group. See
.B ADMINISTRATOR PRIVILEGES
in
.BR euclid-cli-eam (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The role was created; its JSON representation is printed.
.TP
.B 1
No name or no permission given, a permission no module answers, a name
that is already one of your account's roles or one of the built-in ones,
or the caller is not an administrator. If the failure was an expired or
invalid session (\fIHTTP 401\fR), the stored credentials file is removed;
run
.B euclid-cli eam login
again before retrying.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
# What an order service does, and no more
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam create-role \e
    --name order-service \e
    --permission ens:publish-message \e
    --permission eqs:receive-messages \e
    --permission eqs:delete-message \e
    --description "The order service's own rights"

# Everything one module answers, including what it gains later
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam create-role \e
    --name storage-owner --permission 'esm:*'

# Read-only reporting, narrower than the built-in reader
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam create-role \e
    --name reporting -p ens:list-topics -p ens:get-topic-metadata
.fi
.RE
.PP
Quote
.I *
so the shell does not expand it against the working directory.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eam (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-list-permissions (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-list-roles (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-get-role (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-update-role (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-delete-role (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-grant-role (1)
